nology company, focuses on developing therapeutics for patients living with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) and rheumatic diseases. The company develops SPY001, a humanized monoclonal immunoglobulin G1 antibody that is in phase-2 clinical stage designed to bind selectively to the a4\u00df7 integrin being developed for the treatment of IBD (ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease); and SPY002 and SPY072 \u0096 anti-TL1A mAbs designed to bind to tumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A (TL1A). It is also developing SPY003, a clinical-stage program, which is in phase-2 clinical stage designed to bind to interleukin 23 (IL-23); SPY120, a combination of anti-a4\u00df7 and anti-TL1A mAbs; SPY130, a combination anti-a4\u00df7 and anti-IL-23 mAbs; and SPY230, a combination anti-TL1A and anti-IL-23 mAbs. The company was formerly known as Aeglea BioTherapeutics, Inc. and changed its name to Spyre Therapeutics, Inc. in November 2023. The company was incorporated in 2013 and is based in Waltham, Massachusetts.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Cameron  Turtle DPHIL, Ph.D. | CEO & Director | Age 35 | Compensation: $1.2M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 130 people. Stock-based compensation of $289K per employee reflects an ownership culture. Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Spyre Therapeutics, Inc. is a mid-cap healthcare business with a $9.9B market cap on $0 of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -39% over the past three years, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at -74800% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","On the balance sheet, interest coverage of 40\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Pio